<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gruffydd Maelor I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1191">1191</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Gruffydd ap Madog, Gruffudd Maelor I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gruffydd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maelor">Maelor</a> I was a Welsh prince who ruled northern Powys after the death of his father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madog-ap-maredudd">Madog ap Maredudd</a>, the last king of all Powys, in 1160. His territory, centred on Maelor and Ial, became the principality later named Powys Fadog after his son Madog ap Gruffydd Maelor. He married a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-gwynedd">Owain Gwynedd</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gruffydd-maelor-i.jpg" alt="Gruffydd Maelor I" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AlexD · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Gruffydd_Maelor_I_ap_Madog_Prince_of_Powys_Fadog.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Northern Powys became Powys Fadog under <b>Gruffudd Maelor</b>, and southern Powys became Powys Wenwynwyn, named after Owain Cyfeiliog’s son Gwenwynwyn.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-powys">Kingdom of Powys · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The northern part passed to his son <b>Gruffydd Maelor</b> and then to Gruffydd's son Madog ap Gruffydd Maelor, after whom it was named Powys Fadog, covering Maelor, Ial, Edeirnion and Cynllaith.</blockquote><a href="/state/powys-fadog">Powys Fadog</a></li></ul></section>
